Here’s a clean Ubuntu terminal workflow to build and deploy a static website using GitHub and Vercel.

1) Create the project
Open a terminal and run:

```bash
mkdir my-static-site
cd my-static-site
```

Create the website files:

```bash
cat > index.html <<'EOF'
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Registration Form</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Student Registration Form</h1>

    <form>
        <label>Full Name:</label>
        <input type="text" name="name" placeholder="Enter your name">
        <br><br>

        <label>Email:</label>
        <input type="email" name="email" placeholder="Enter your email">
        <br><br>

        <label>Password:</label>
        <input type="password" name="password" placeholder="Enter password">
        <br><br>

        <label>Phone Number:</label>
        <input type="tel" name="phone">
        <br><br>

        <label>Gender:</label>
        <input type="radio" name="gender" value="Male"> Male
        <input type="radio" name="gender" value="Female"> Female
        <br><br>

        <label>Date of Birth:</label>
        <input type="date" name="dob">
        <br><br>

        <label>Course:</label>
        <select name="course">
            <option value="">Select Course</option>
            <option value="BCA">BCA</option>
            <option value="BBA">BBA</option>
            <option value="BCOM">BCOM</option>
        </select>
        <br><br>

        <label>Address:</label>
        <textarea name="address" rows="4" cols="30"></textarea>
        <br><br>

        <input type="checkbox" name="terms">
        I agree to the terms and conditions.
        <br><br>

        <input type="submit" value="Register">
        <input type="reset" value="Clear">

    </form>

</body>
</html>
```
EOF
```

2) Initialize Git and push to GitHub
If Git is not installed:

```bash
sudo apt update
sudo apt install git -y
```

Now initialize the repo and commit:

```bash
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial static website"
```

Create a GitHub repo:
If you have GitHub CLI installed:

```bash
gh auth login
gh repo create my-static-site --public --source=. --remote=origin --push
```

If `gh` is not installed:

```bash
sudo apt install gh -y
gh auth login
gh repo create my-static-site --public --source=. --remote=origin --push
```

If you prefer GitHub web UI:
- Create a repo on GitHub
- Then connect it:

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/my-static-site.git
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main
```

3) Deploy with Vercel
Option A: Use Vercel web dashboard
- Go to https://vercel.com
- Sign in with GitHub
- Click “Add New Project”
- Import your GitHub repo
- Keep default settings
- Click Deploy

For a plain static site, Vercel usually auto-detects it and works without a build step.

Option B: Use Vercel CLI in Ubuntu terminal

Install Vercel CLI:

```bash
npm install -g vercel
```

Then from your project folder:

```bash
vercel login
vercel --prod
```

Follow the prompts:
- Select your GitHub account
- Choose the repo
- Confirm project settings
- Vercel will deploy it and give you a live URL

4) Verify deployment
After deployment, Vercel gives you a generated URL like:

```text
https://my-static-site-xxxxx.vercel.app
```

Open it in your browser. Your static site should load.

5) Optional: Keep updating the site
After making changes:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Update website"
git push origin main
```

Vercel will automatically redeploy the project when the GitHub repo updates.

Notes
- No backend is required for a static website.
- Vercel is ideal for static HTML/CSS/JS projects.
- If you want a more advanced website later, you can use Vite, Next.js, or React, but plain HTML/CSS/JS works perfectly for static hosting.

If you want, I can also give you:
- a version using Vite
- a portfolio site template
- a landing page template
- a full command script you can paste directly into Ubuntu terminal
